# phone: pjsua configuration -- TLS signalling, mandatory SRTP media
#
# Syntax: same as the pjsua command line, one option per line, '#' comments.
# Options that take a value are written as `--name value`.
#
# Do not edit a copy of this file by hand and expect it to work: paths must be
# absolute (pjsua does not expand `~` or environment variables). Run
#
#     phone sip init
#
# which renders @HOME@ into your real home directory, installs this file at
# ~/.config/phone/sip.conf with mode 0600, and leaves the original as a
# reference. The launcher refuses to start while placeholders remain.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# transport: TLS only, and only on one port
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pjsua derives the TLS listener port as (local port + 1) -- see
# pjsip-apps/src/pjsua/pjsua_app.c: "Set TLS port as TCP port+1",
# tcp_cfg.port++; pjsua_transport_create(PJSIP_TRANSPORT_TLS, ...).
#
# So local port 5060 => TLS on 5061, the standard SIP-over-TLS port. Setting
# --local-port 5061 here would silently put TLS on 5062 and break both your
# firewall rules and your provider's expectations. `phone sip --check` verifies
# this arithmetic and, when ss(8) is available, verifies the port that is
# actually bound at runtime.
--local-port 5060

# No plaintext transports. With UDP and TCP disabled the TLS listener on 5061
# is the only socket pjsua opens for signalling.
--no-udp
--no-tcp
--use-tls

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# trust: verify the provider, present a client certificate
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PJLIB's OpenSSL backend does NOT use the system trust store: "OpenSSL trusts
# only the CA certificates the application supplies" (PJSIP docs, SSL/TLS
# guide). Omitting --tls-ca-file together with --tls-verify-server means
# verification silently has nothing to verify against -- so both are required
# here, and the launcher enforces them.
--tls-ca-file /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
--tls-verify-server

# Client credentials. Create them with `phone sip init` (sip/gen-certs.sh).
# The private key must be mode 0600; the launcher refuses otherwise.
--tls-cert-file @HOME@/.config/phone/tls/client.pem
--tls-privkey-file @HOME@/.config/phone/tls/client.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# media: SRTP is mandatory, not opportunistic
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --use-srtp 2  = PJMEDIA_SRTP_MANDATORY: a call proceeds only if secure media
#                 can be established. 1 would be "optional", which downgrades
#                 silently when the peer does not offer SRTP.
# --srtp-secure 1 = SRTP requires secure signalling (TLS). Do not set 2 unless
#                 your account URIs use the sips: scheme -- with plain sip:
#                 URIs pjsua refuses to set up media at all.
--use-srtp 2
--srtp-secure 1

# Media port base. Keep this range aligned with the firewall rules
# (firewall/lockdown.sh defaults to 4000-4010, the default pjsua window).
--rtp-port 4000

# DTLS-SRTP key agreement puts no key material in the SDP; SDES puts the SRTP
# keys in the SDP, protected only by the TLS signalling channel. Prefer DTLS
# when the provider supports it. Only available in newer pjproject builds --
# check with: pjsua --help | grep srtp-keying
#--srtp-keying 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# logging: never dump SIP messages
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Level 5 (pjsua's default) prints full SIP messages, including the SDP that
# carries SDES SRTP key material, to your log. Level 3 keeps signalling state
# visible without ever writing message bodies or key material to disk.
--log-level 3
--app-log-level 3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# your provider -- must be edited before first use
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every URI that should go over TLS must say so: `;transport=tls`. Without it
# pjsua resolves the URI for UDP, finds no UDP transport, and fails.
#
# Replace the three placeholders below, or put your credentials in
# ~/.config/phone/sip.secrets and source them (see docs/PROVIDERS.md).
--registrar sip:sip.YOUR_PROVIDER.example:5061\;transport=tls
--id sip:YOUR_SIP_USERNAME@sip.YOUR_PROVIDER.example
--realm YOUR_PROVIDER.example
--username YOUR_SIP_USERNAME
--password YOUR_SIP_PASSWORD
--contact sip:YOUR_SIP_USERNAME@sip.YOUR_PROVIDER.example:5061\;transport=tls

# Keep the registration refresh polite; 300s is well inside most providers'
# expiry windows and cuts keepalive chatter by ~5x on the default 55s.
--reg-timeout 300

# Do not send STUN queries to third parties (a metadata leak: the STUN server
# learns your address, and it is not needed when registering to a provider).
# --stun-srv and --use-ice are deliberately absent.
--auto-update-nat 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# optional: run headless, no sound card, media still flows
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#--null-audio
